  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of constituents for enterprise development in Guyana</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The history of cooperatives in Guyana dates back to the 1830s, with formerly enslaved persons forming the first cooperatives after the abolishment of slavery. Despite initial failures due to financial and infrastructural challenges, the cooperative ethos persisted, leading to the establishment of the Cooperative Republic in 1970. Hundreds of cooperative societies were formed in various sectors, supported by institutions like the Guyana National Cooperative Bank and the Kuru Kuru Cooperative College. However, economic decline in the 1980s, exacerbated by factors like falling sugar prices and outward migration, led to a reduction in the number of cooperatives.

Recently, there has been renewed interest in the Social and Solidarity Economy, with initiatives funded by the Caribbean Development Fund. The Ministry of Labour requested technical support from the ILO to further promote cooperativism, particularly in rural areas. The ILO, in collaboration with Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) under a project called &quot;FISH4ACP&quot;, aims to enhance cooperative functionality and foster business development in informal groups, focusing on women in the Atlantic Seabob Sector. This collaboration follows a value chain assesment by FAO which chose the Atlantic Seabob sector based on its high potential as a domestic preferred product and for its high export market potential. This collaboration seeks to strengthen governance, leadership skills, and economic resilience in Guyana&apos;s fisheries sector by 2032. The ILO will be working with Ministry of Labour&apos;s Cooperative and Friendly Societies Department, as well as with the Ministry of Agriculture and its Fisheries department for the benefit of fishers and rural women operating in the value chain and their organization.
FISH4ACP is an initiative of the Organisation of African, Caribbean and Pacific States (OACPS) aimed at making fisheries and aquaculture value chains in Africa, the Caribbean and the Pacific more productive.</narrative>
  </description>
